  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1029 PM MDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nHOT WITH MOUNTAIN STORMS ON FRIDAY: \n\nHigh pressure remains anchored over the Four Corners region on \nFriday, providing another day of scattered showers and \nthunderstorms mainly over the high terrain. The main concerns \nwith any storm activity remain gusty outflow winds to 35 mph and\nlightning with some localized wetting rain in the stronger \nconvection. While hydro concerns will be minimal through Friday \ndue to the drier low levels, stronger convection could result in\na localized flash flooding threat over burn scars and \nsusceptible terrain. \n\nAs we close out the work week, unseasonably warm temperatures \nremain in place with triple digits possible in many desert \nvalley locations. Elsewhere, look for temps to remain about 10 \ndegrees above normal until we hit the weekend.\n\nWET WEATHER RETURNS THIS WEEKEND:\n\nWetter weather returns to the region on Saturday as low \npressure pushes inland across the Pacific Northwest and nudges \nthe ridge overhead eastward, putting eastern Utah and western \nColorado in a more favorable trajectory for monsoonal moisture. \nWhile the strongest fetch of moisture will remain further west \nacross the Great Basin, PWATs will increase to 150-175% of \nnormal. Flash flooding impacts become more probable during this \nperiod as a robust shortwave moves through the southerly flow \nlate Saturday afternoon into the evening. This added forcing, \ncombined with the uptick in moisture, will likely result in \nwidespread storm activity late in the day on Saturday, \npotentially continuing overnight.\n\nConvection continues on Sunday with a moisture remaining in \nplace. Without stronger synoptic forcing, scattered thunderstorm\ndevelopment favoring the higher terrain is anticipated. As \nguidance stands now, the potential for flash flooding remains \nelevated over the burn scars, but lower than Saturday's \npotential. \n\nModel guidance supports a decrease in moisture by the start of \nthe new work week, but with PWATs remaining above 100% of \nnormal, afternoon convection will likely continue over the \nhigher terrain each afternoon. An early look at temperatures \nshows afternoon highs remaining 5-10 degrees above normal \nthrough the seven-day forecast.",
